{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-126101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{5n-1}(x-1)^{n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=1$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{5n-1}(x-1)^{n}=(x-1)+\\frac{4}{9}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{7}(x-1)^{3}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{8}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{6}{7}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126101" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "48eb5ac7cd0d8aa3dbba99a9455a70f81b1e518a36606cd9f2fe6a9ff3a4483d"} {"question" "q-126101" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126101" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{4}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{16}{81}(x-1)^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{4}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{3}{7}(x-1)^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{8}{3}(x-1)+\\frac{6}{5}(x-1)^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{8}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{16}{27}(x-1)^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{8}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{6}{7}(x-1)^{2}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126101" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{8}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{6}{7}(x-1)^{2}+⋯"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-50867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n-1}\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n}}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-50867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n-1}\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=x-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-⋯.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-50867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "48eb5ac7cd0d8aa3dbba99a9455a70f81b1e518a36606cd9f2fe6a9ff3a4483d"} {"question" "q-50867" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-50867" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+⋯"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(x)=-x+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+⋯"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-⋯"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(x)=x-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-⋯"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-50867" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{f}^{′}(x)=x-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-⋯"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-347641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}(x-2)^{n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=2$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-2)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-2)^{2}+⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-347641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}(x-2)^{n}=\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{6}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}(x-2)^{3}-⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)+\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{2}+⋯.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-347641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "48eb5ac7cd0d8aa3dbba99a9455a70f81b1e518a36606cd9f2fe6a9ff3a4483d"} {"question" "q-347641" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-347641" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-347641" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-347641" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-347641" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-347641" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{(n+1)!}{x}^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\,⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{(n+1)!}{x}^{n+1}=2x-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{4}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2-2x+{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "48eb5ac7cd0d8aa3dbba99a9455a70f81b1e518a36606cd9f2fe6a9ff3a4483d"} {"question" "q-352355" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352355" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2-2x+x^2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{2}^{2n+1}}{(2n)!}(x+2)^{n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=-2$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x+2)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x+2)^{2}+⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{2}^{2n+1}}{(2n)!}(x+2)^{n}=4(x+2)-\\frac{4}{3}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{8}{45}(x+2)^{3}-⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4-\\frac{8}{3}(x+2)+\\frac{8}{15}(x+2)^{2}+⋯.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "48eb5ac7cd0d8aa3dbba99a9455a70f81b1e518a36606cd9f2fe6a9ff3a4483d"} {"question" "q-352512" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352512" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352512" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352512" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{8}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352512" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352512" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{8}{15}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-83463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{n(x-3)^{n}}{n+3}.\n$$\n\nFind ${a}_{n},$ given that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}{a}_{n}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-83463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-3)-\\frac{2}{5}(x-3)^{2}+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{n(x-3)^{n}}{n+3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{4}{5}(x-3)+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-83463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "48eb5ac7cd0d8aa3dbba99a9455a70f81b1e518a36606cd9f2fe6a9ff3a4483d"} {"question" "q-83463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-83463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}"] ["math" "{a}_{n}=\\frac{n(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}"] ["math" "{a}_{n}=\\frac{n(x-3)^{n}}{n+3}"] ["math" "{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}"] ["math" "{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n}}{n+3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-83463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{(2n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}\n$(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{120}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{(2n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{2x}{120}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}n{x}^{n-1}}{(2n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}}{(2n+1)!} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{(2n+1)!}(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "48eb5ac7cd0d8aa3dbba99a9455a70f81b1e518a36606cd9f2fe6a9ff3a4483d"} {"question" "q-352520" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352520" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{n}{(2n+1)!}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-83461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}\\frac{(x+2)^{n+1}}{n!}.\n$$\n\nFind ${a}_{n},$ given that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}{a}_{n}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-83461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2!}(x+2)^{3}-⋯+{{(-1)^{n}\\frac{(x+2)^{n+1}}{n!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-2(x+2)+\\frac{3}{2!}(x+2)^{2}-⋯+{{(-1)^{n}\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n!}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n!}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-83461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "48eb5ac7cd0d8aa3dbba99a9455a70f81b1e518a36606cd9f2fe6a9ff3a4483d"} {"question" "q-83461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-83461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{n}=(n+1)(x+2)^{n}"] ["math" "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n+1}}{n}"] ["math" "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n!}"] ["math" "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n}"] ["math" "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(x+2)^{n+1}}{(n-1)!}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-83461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n!}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-50870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k+1}.\n$$\n\nFind ${a}_{k},$ given that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{k}(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-50870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2(x-1)^{2}+4(x-1)^{3}-⋯+{{(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k+1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }k\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-4(x-1)+12(x-1)^{2}-⋯+{{(k+1)(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }k\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }(k+1)(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}=k+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-50870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "48eb5ac7cd0d8aa3dbba99a9455a70f81b1e518a36606cd9f2fe6a9ff3a4483d"} {"question" "q-50870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-50870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{k}=2k-1"] ["math" "{a}_{k}=3k+2"] ["math" "{a}_{k}=k+1"] ["math" "{a}_{k}=k+2"] ["math" "{a}_{k}=k-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-50870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{k}=k+1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{n}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯+{{\\frac{2\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x+2{x}^{2}+⋯+{{\\frac{2n(n+1){x}^{n}}{(n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(n+1){x}^{n}}{(n+1)!} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n!}{x}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "48eb5ac7cd0d8aa3dbba99a9455a70f81b1e518a36606cd9f2fe6a9ff3a4483d"} {"question" "q-352524" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352524" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{n!}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-50912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{2}^{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-50912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{2}^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|2x| \\\\  & =2|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-50912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "48eb5ac7cd0d8aa3dbba99a9455a70f81b1e518a36606cd9f2fe6a9ff3a4483d"} {"question" "q-50912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-50912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\infty "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-50912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{2}^{2n}(n!)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2n+2}}{{2}^{2n+2}((n+1)!)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{2n}(n!)^{2}}{{x}^{2n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}(n+1)^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ Since $0<1$ for all $x,$ we conclude that $R=\\infty .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126166" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "48eb5ac7cd0d8aa3dbba99a9455a70f81b1e518a36606cd9f2fe6a9ff3a4483d"} {"question" "q-126166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\infty "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126166" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{2}^{n}n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n+1}(n+1)}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n}n}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{xn}{2(n+1)}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{2} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "48eb5ac7cd0d8aa3dbba99a9455a70f81b1e518a36606cd9f2fe6a9ff3a4483d"} {"question" "q-126168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\infty "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(4n-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$\n\nThe radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(4(n+1)-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n+1}}{(4n-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{4n+3}{4n-1}||\\frac{x}{4}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{4} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "48eb5ac7cd0d8aa3dbba99a9455a70f81b1e518a36606cd9f2fe6a9ff3a4483d"} {"question" "q-352603" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-352603" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\sqrt{n}\\,{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\sqrt{n+1}\\,{x}^{n+1}}{\\sqrt{n}\\,{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|x\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{n}}| \\\\  & =|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "48eb5ac7cd0d8aa3dbba99a9455a70f81b1e518a36606cd9f2fe6a9ff3a4483d"} {"question" "q-126169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\infty "] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "q-126169" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f11d495cadf8a5afe06ed8a28a86f2a02280e19fba475574da3bd49513f90d2"} {"question" "e-762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty { {{n}}(x-1)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=1$ of $f'(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/example-762.json" "file_sha256" "3280579377da59b0b614fbe5a7dd78cca7ad53a40b17839e99d3f0d8fd108426"} {"question" "e-762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty { {{n}}(x-1)^{n}} = (x-1) + {2(x-1)^2} + {3(x-1)^3} +\\cdots .\n$$\n\nTo find $f'(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+0+4(x-1)+9(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+4(x-1)+9(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/example-762.json" "file_sha256" "3280579377da59b0b614fbe5a7dd78cca7ad53a40b17839e99d3f0d8fd108426"} {"question" "e-763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{ (x-3)^{n}}{n}\n$$\n\nFind $a_n,$ given that\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/example-763.json" "file_sha256" "cbf0504e5521cb8eabe1d5f8dde024bb1881dc8cc5ebf29b162af093d93584a0"} {"question" "e-763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/assets/7316ce9632bc2ab0347accb71c4c0dc90db8a87e0f9bead704e0218523a9b367.png)\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/assets/846a66292603d3436219826be2e9419b5798fa121341d1e1676fba63c0f37eec.png)\nWe can now write the Taylor series for $f'(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (x-3)^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\na_n = (x-3)^{n-1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/example-763.json" "file_sha256" "cbf0504e5521cb8eabe1d5f8dde024bb1881dc8cc5ebf29b162af093d93584a0"} {"question" "e-764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$  about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-3)^{n}\\,x^n}{n^3}\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x| <            R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/example-764.json" "file_sha256" "7adf4fa22e354e79f3ac21cc3698dc3f5ccd056423c0ac29e95da90a69e26e39"} {"question" "e-764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of $f'(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of $f'(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3}}{(-3)^{n}\\,{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)x\\cdot {n}^{3}}{(n+1)^{3}}| \\\\  & =3|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{3}} \\\\  & =3|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R = \\dfrac 1 3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/example-764.json" "file_sha256" "7adf4fa22e354e79f3ac21cc3698dc3f5ccd056423c0ac29e95da90a69e26e39"}] "usage" {"e-762" [] "e-763" [] "e-764" []} "basis" 1556 "retractions" [[17592186054494 :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n$\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty {{{n}}(x-1)^{n}}$.\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=1$ of $f'(x)$."] [17592186054494 :question/worked-solution "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty {{{n}}(x-1)^{n}} = (x-1) + {2(x-1)^2} + {3(x-1)^3} +\\cdots\n$$\n\nTo find $f'(x)$, we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 1 + 0 + 4(x - 1) + 9(x - 1)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 + 4(x - 1) + 9(x - 1)^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186054495 :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n$\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(x-3)^{n}}{n}$\nFind $a_n$, given that\n\n$f'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n$."] [17592186054495 :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nn\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$n$\nWe can now write the Taylor series for $f'(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (x-3)^{n-1}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\na_n = (x-3)^{n-1}\n$$"] [17592186054496 :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n$\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-3)^{n}\\,x^n}{n^3}$\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $\\mid x \\mid < R$, where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, about $x=0$."] [17592186054496 :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of $f'(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x)$. So, to find the radius of convergence of $f'(x)$, we need to compute $R$.\n\nTo calculate $R$, we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid \\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} \\mid \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid ((-3)^{n + 1}x^{n + 1})/((n + 1)^{3}) \\cdot (n^{3})/((-3)^{n}x^{n}) \\mid \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞) \\mid ((-3)x \\cdot n^{3})/((n + 1)^{3}) \\mid \\\\\n&= 3\\begin{vmatrix}x & lim_(n → ∞)(1)/((1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{3}) \\\\ = 3 & x\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges when $L<1$. So, it converges when\n\n$$\n3 \\mid x \\mid \\mid < 1; \\mid x \\mid \\mid < \\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is\n\n$$\nR = \\dfrac 1 3\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
